# Documentation Standards

These standards ensure code is auditable and maintainable by third parties. Well-documented code reduces onboarding time, prevents security oversights, and enables effective code reviews.

## 1. JSDoc Requirements

ALL exported functions MUST have JSDoc documentation.

### Required Tags

| Tag | Description | Required |
|-----|-------------|----------|
| `@param` | Document each parameter with type and purpose | Always |
| `@returns` | Document return type and what it represents | Always |
| `@throws` | Document errors/exceptions that can be thrown | When applicable |
| `@example` | Show usage example | Recommended |
| `@security` | Note security considerations | For security-sensitive code |
| `@audit` | Reference audit requirements | When audit trail needed |

### Complete JSDoc Template

```typescript
/**
 * Brief description of what the function does.
 * 
 * Additional context explaining why this function exists and
 * any important behavior details.
 * 
 * @param userId - The unique identifier of the user
 * @param options - Configuration options for the operation
 * @param options.includeDeleted - Whether to include soft-deleted records
 * @param options.limit - Maximum number of results to return
 * 
 * @returns Object containing the results and any errors
 * @returns.data - The fetched records, or null if error
 * @returns.error - Error message if operation failed, null otherwise
 * 
 * @throws {AuthenticationError} When user is not authenticated
 * @throws {ValidationError} When userId format is invalid
 * 
 * @security Validates user has permission to access requested data
 * @audit Creates audit log entry for data access
 * 
 * @example
 * ```ts
 * const { data, error } = await fetchUserData(userId, { limit: 10 })
 * if (error) {
 *   console.error('Failed to fetch:', error)
 *   return
 * }
 * ```
 */
export async function fetchUserData(
  userId: string,
  options?: { includeDeleted?: boolean; limit?: number }
): Promise<{ data: UserRecord[] | null; error: string | null }> {
  // Implementation
}
```

## 2. Security Annotations

Use inline comments with specific prefixes for security-critical code. These make security logic immediately visible during code review.

### Annotation Prefixes

| Prefix | Use For | Example |
|--------|---------|---------|
| `// SECURITY:` | Security-critical logic, attack prevention | `// SECURITY: Prevents timing attacks` |
| `// AUTH:` | Authentication and authorization checks | `// AUTH: Verify user owns this resource` |
| `// AUDIT:` | Audit trail and logging requirements | `// AUDIT: Log all payment events` |
| `// RLS:` | Row Level Security policy explanations | `// RLS: Users can only see own data` |
| `// VALIDATION:` | Input validation and sanitization | `// VALIDATION: Sanitize user HTML input` |

### Security Annotation Examples

```typescript
// SECURITY: Atomic idempotency check - prevents race conditions where
// the same webhook could be processed multiple times concurrently
const { acquired, alreadyProcessed } = await acquireEventLock(event)

// AUTH: Verify the requesting user owns this resource before allowing access
// This prevents IDOR attacks where attackers guess resource IDs
const { data: resource } = await supabase
  .from('resources')
  .select('*')
  .eq('id', resourceId)
  .eq('user_id', user.id)  // AUTH: Ownership check
  .single()

// AUDIT: Log all payment state changes for compliance and debugging
console.log('Payment state transition:', {
  orderId,
  previousStatus: order.status,
  newStatus: 'paid',
  timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
})

// RLS: This policy ensures users can only access their own foundry's data.
// The foundry_id check is critical for multi-tenant isolation.
await supabase
  .from('foundry_settings')
  .select('*')
  .eq('foundry_id', user.foundry_id)

// VALIDATION: Sanitize file path to prevent directory traversal attacks
// User input could contain "../" sequences to access unauthorized files
const safePath = path.normalize(userPath).replace(/^(\.\.[\/\\])+/, '')

// SECURITY: Verify payment amount matches database to prevent amount tampering
// Attackers could modify client-side amounts before submission
const expectedAmount = Math.round(Number(orderValidation.total_amount) * 100)
if (Math.abs(paymentIntent.amount - expectedAmount) > 1) {
  console.error('[SECURITY] Payment amount mismatch!', {
    expected: expectedAmount,
    received: paymentIntent.amount,
  })
}
```

