## 4. Component Documentation Template

For React components, include this documentation structure:

```tsx
/**
 * OrderSummaryCard - Displays order details with payment status
 * 
 * Purpose:
 * Shows a summary of an order including items, pricing, and current
 * status. Used in both buyer and seller views of order management.
 * 
 * Props:
 * @param order - The order object containing all order details
 * @param showSellerActions - Whether to show seller-specific action buttons
 * @param onStatusChange - Callback when order status is updated
 * 
 * State Management:
 * - Local state for UI interactions (expanded sections, loading states)
 * - Order data comes from parent, mutations trigger onStatusChange callback
 * - Uses optimistic updates for better UX on status changes
 * 
 * @example
 * ```tsx
 * <OrderSummaryCard
 *   order={orderData}
 *   showSellerActions={isSeller}
 *   onStatusChange={handleStatusChange}
 * />
 * ```
 */
interface OrderSummaryCardProps {
  order: Order
  showSellerActions?: boolean
  onStatusChange?: (orderId: string, newStatus: OrderStatus) => Promise<void>
}

export function OrderSummaryCard({
  order,
  showSellerActions = false,
  onStatusChange,
}: OrderSummaryCardProps) {
  // Component implementation
}
```

## 5. File Header Template

For complex files (>200 lines, critical business logic, or security-sensitive), include a file header:

```typescript
/**
 * @file Stripe Webhook Handler
 * 
 * Purpose:
 * Handles incoming Stripe webhook events for payment processing,
 * account updates, and dispute management. This is a critical
 * financial pathway - changes require security review.
 * 
 * Key Responsibilities:
 * - Verify webhook signatures (security-critical)
 * - Process payment events with idempotency
 * - Handle disputes and refunds
 * - Update order and account statuses
 * 
 * Security Considerations:
 * - All events verified via Stripe signature
 * - Atomic idempotency prevents double-processing
 * - Payment amounts validated against database
 * - Generic error responses prevent information leakage
 * 
 * Related Files:
 * - src/lib/stripe/client.ts - Stripe client configuration
 * - src/lib/payments/flow.ts - Payment confirmation logic
 * - src/types/billing.ts - Billing type definitions
 * 
 * @see https://stripe.com/docs/webhooks for Stripe webhook documentation
 * @see src/actions/billing.ts for related billing actions
 */
```

## 6. When Documentation is Required

| Code Type | Documentation Required | Notes |
|-----------|----------------------|-------|
| Exported functions | **Always** | Full JSDoc with all applicable tags |
| Complex internal functions | Yes | At minimum: brief description and param types |
| Security-sensitive code | **Always** | Must include `// SECURITY:` or `// AUTH:` annotation |
| Business rules | **Always** | Explain the "why" with reference to requirements if available |
| Type assertions (`as Type`) | Yes | Explain why the assertion is safe |
| Workarounds/hacks | Yes | Include ticket reference for proper fix |
| Non-obvious algorithms | Yes | Explain the approach and any edge cases |
| Database queries with complex conditions | Yes | Explain what data is being fetched and why |
| Error handling decisions | Yes | Explain why errors are handled this way |

### Type Assertion Documentation

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Unexplained type assertion
const user = data as User

// ✅ GOOD: Justified type assertion
// Safe assertion: query filters ensure only User records returned,
// and we've validated data exists before this point
const user = data as User

// ✅ BETTER: Avoid assertion with runtime check
if (!isUser(data)) {
  throw new Error('Invalid user data structure')
}
const user = data
```

### Workaround Documentation

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Undocumented workaround
// @ts-nocheck

// ✅ GOOD: Documented workaround with ticket
// @ts-nocheck - Billing tables exist but types not regenerated after 
// billing_enhancements migration. Remove after running `npm run db:types`
// Ticket: TECH-DEBT-456
```

## 7. Documentation Quality Checklist

Before submitting code for review, verify:

### JSDoc Completeness
- [ ] All exported functions have JSDoc
- [ ] All parameters documented with types and purpose
- [ ] Return type documented with meaning
- [ ] Throws documented for functions that can throw

### Security Documentation
- [ ] Security-critical code has `// SECURITY:` annotations
- [ ] Auth checks have `// AUTH:` annotations
- [ ] Validation logic has `// VALIDATION:` annotations
- [ ] RLS-related code has `// RLS:` annotations

### Business Logic
- [ ] Business rules explained with "why" not "what"
- [ ] Non-obvious decisions documented
- [ ] Edge cases explained
- [ ] External references (tickets, PRDs) included where relevant

### Code Clarity
- [ ] Complex algorithms explained
- [ ] Type assertions justified
- [ ] Workarounds documented with ticket references
- [ ] No magic numbers (constants documented)

